00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:37.924
sell more Marvel merch,

00:07:37.924 --> 00:07:40.960
sell more music records,
and they're not totally wrong about that.

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:45.164
The character was literally created
as a joint venture like corporate synergy

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:49.402
quote unquote, between Marvel Comics
and Casablanca Records, a music company.

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:52.905
the idea was, is that Marvel Comics
would write stories for the character

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:56.409
like you would normally do for comic book
characters, and they would also create

00:07:56.409 --> 00:07:59.979
real life albums as if the character
were making their own albums.

00:08:00.179 --> 00:08:01.280
So you'd buy the Dazzler

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.550
comic book, learn about her story,
but then you'd buy the Dazzler album

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:08.221
in real life, which cost way more money,
making them way more money.

00:08:08.488 --> 00:08:11.357
Eventually their corporate partnership
ended, and Marvel stopped writing

00:08:11.357 --> 00:08:12.391
Casablanca Records

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:14.494
into the Dazzler comics,
and she just became

00:08:14.494 --> 00:08:16.395
more of an actual comic book character.

00:08:16.395 --> 00:08:19.999
but it was an early example of Marvel
trying to do these cross-platform media

00:08:19.999 --> 00:08:21.701
integrations across comics.

00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:24.103
Eventually they got better at doing stuff
like this.

00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:26.072
early on, the most famous example of this

00:08:26.072 --> 00:08:29.775
was just a few years later
in the 1980s version of Secret Wars.

00:08:29.809 --> 00:08:32.912
It was kind of like Shadows of the Empire
was for Star Wars in the nineties.

00:08:33.045 --> 00:08:36.482
Like we don't have a movie coming out, but
we want to sell a bunch of merchandise.

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:40.019
